./LOGBOOK.md:2964:### 2026-08-22 — TASK-260822-1f533i core.md 4.1.1 script-worker-v1 prose: reviewer verdict cycle 2, ACCEPTED
./LOGBOOK.md:2966:- BLOCKING ITEM FROM CYCLE 1 IS GENUINELY CLOSED: the script inventory's Linux `active-process-count-limit` moved from `available: RLIMIT_NPROC` to `host-conditional: delegated cgroup v2 pids.max`, so the section's own "an `available` control MUST report `applied`" no longer forces every conforming Linux manager to assert a private aggregate descendant bound it does not have. The implementer went past the literal ask with a normative paragraph stating that `RLIMIT_NPROC`/`RLIMIT_AS` are not private aggregate domains and MUST NOT back either aggregate control on any platform — that converts three previously *asserted* inventory verdicts into a stated argument, which is what stops the next editor reverting the cell. Worth copying as a pattern: when a review forces a cell change, write the reason into the normative text, not only into the commit message.
./LOGBOOK.md:3006:- CI FINDING — CURRENT PINS ARE FALSE-GREEN, NOT SCHEMA-8-QUALIFIED: the exact `curator-spec/.github/workflows/implementations.yml` commands were run against the active Schema 8 script-worker candidate root (manifest SHA-256 `9dc1c659697f7218dd844863cef08255a4526b4ab04bdcdb6cfc7d21e3784322`). Curator pin `bd6ba08` passed at exit 0 because its generic-looking schema test switches only on the two Schema 6 names; CocoaSkills pin `6fc2fd9` passed at exit 0 (106 passed) because it never reads `schema-cases/index.json`. Green pinned jobs therefore do not prove that a pin consumes Schema 8.
